{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSnJydpdFtjRypZDcNfu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draw anything, this is how I read the request. A yes moves the work on to the model and writes nothing.\n\nQuestion: which screens does a customer pass through to start a return in the app, and where do they choose?\nOutcome: a reader can follow a return from the order list to the QR confirmation and see where the 30-day window ends it.\nReader: Fernhouse product and support teams.\n\nSettled by the sources:\n- Screens and their order, from your description: order list, order detail with Start a return, return reasons, the drop-off or pickup choice, and the confirmation with a QR code\n- The 30-day window and its Contact support exit, from the returns doc, as current behavior. The context page's glossary also gives the return window as 30 days from delivery\n\nLeft out:\n- The free pickup for orders over 100 euros, a proposal under review\n- Anything after the confirmation, and any address, slot or refund step, because none was described\n\nAssumed, each with what would settle it:\n- The in-app return is live now. The context page says there was no self-serve return on 2026-09-18, so the Post-purchase team or the release notes of the app version with these screens would settle it. If the screens have not shipped, the subtitle and source line say so\n- The 30-day check sits at the order detail. The order detail screenshot or the doc section that shows where Contact support appears would settle it\n- Labels use your wording. The screenshots would settle it\n- Balanced detail, mixed audience, laptop reading distance\n\nOutline, in drawing order:\n1. The question the board answers: which screens does a customer pass through to start a return in the app, and where do they choose?\n2. Order list, where the flow starts\n3. Order detail\n4. Within 30 days of delivery?, a choice\n5. Contact support, an end beside the main line\n6. Return reasons\n7. Drop-off or pickup?, a choice\n8. Confirmation with QR code, the goal\n\nConnectors:\n- Order list to Order detail, and Order detail to the 30-day choice\n- 30-day choice to Contact support, labelled No\n- 30-day choice to Return reasons, labelled \"Yes, Start a return\"\n- Return reasons to the method choice\n- Method choice to the confirmation twice, labelled Drop-off point and Courier pickup\n\nThat is 7 nodes and 7 connectors, under the budget of 9. Every node and connector would be missed if cut.\n\nTwo framings:\n- A, recommended: the happy path on one straight line with Contact support beside it. It shows the route and the one way out. It does not show other reasons a return could fail\n- B: a state map of a return, with an open window, a closed window and a requested return. It shows eligibility at a glance. It drops the screens your question asks about, and the sources give no events for the states\n\nLook, all proposed:\n- Colour marks the goal, the confirmation with the QR code. Contact support is an exit, not a failure\n- Three zones: Find the order (order list, order detail), Request the return (the 30-day choice, Contact support, reasons, method choice) and Done (confirmation)\n- Title: Start a return. Subtitle: How a Fernhouse customer starts a return in the app, from the order list to a confirmed return. Source line: Sources: the app and the returns doc as described. Left out: free pickup over 100 euros, a proposal under review\n- Where the eye lands first is the green confirmation at the end of the straight line, which is the node the board most needs read\n\nIs this the right reading of the board?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4546,"cache_read_input_tokens":101152,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4546},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference